CAPE: Selective Predictive Memory for Embodied Agents

A Decision-Theoretic Framework for Calibrated, Memory-Augmented Prediction

Author: Ouadi Maakoul
Version: 4.4 — Final Theoretical Thesis
Status: Theoretical Framework and Formal Analysis
Date: September 2026

---

Abstract

This thesis presents CAPE (Selective Predictive Memory), a formal framework for memory-augmented prediction in embodied agents that treats memory as an optional predictive intervention. Unlike prior memory architectures that co-train memory with the base model or retrieve based on surface similarity, CAPE operates as a frozen middleware layer with three defining properties: (1) the base world model and policy remain unmodified—no gradient updates are required; (2) retrieval is driven by estimated predictive benefit, defined as the expected reduction in decision loss from using a memory; and (3) a calibrated selective gating mechanism ensures that memory is used only when there is positive statistical evidence of benefit, with a formal finite-sample selective risk-control guarantee under exchangeability assumptions.

We define the Selective Predictive Memory (SPM) problem as a constrained optimization over a memory policy that maps queries to memory selections and activation decisions. The theoretical contribution consists of three layers: (A) selective risk control via conformal risk control, guaranteeing bounded expected loss increase on the selected population; (B) prediction-error propagation under contraction assumptions; and (C) closed-loop control cost bounds under prediction-Lipschitz controller sensitivity. The architectural contribution includes a two-tier retrieval system with a unified benefit gate for all memory candidates, a lightweight correction network with relative capacity constraints, a learned benefit estimator trained with explicit exploration to mitigate selection bias, and a conformal calibration module with isolated data buffers.

The central hypothesis is that selective, benefit-gated memory can improve long-horizon autonomy without modifying the base model, while provably limiting harmful memory activation. The framework is validated through formal proofs and a detailed experimental protocol designed to test the theoretical claims in simulation and on real robots.

Keywords: embodied AI, memory-augmented prediction, conformal risk control, selective prediction, world models, robot learning.

