Chapter 1: Introduction

1.1 The Problem

Long-horizon robot manipulation remains challenging because learned world models and policies fail under novel physical conditions, partial observability, and contact-rich dynamics. Recent literature has established memory as a critical component: memory-augmented architectures have demonstrated significant improvements in manipulation benchmarks, with some systems achieving over 96% success on standard benchmarks and over 84% on real-robot tasks. Yet a fundamental problem persists: existing memory systems are typically co-trained with the base policy, retrieved by surface similarity, and applied without explicit mechanisms for detecting when memory is harmful.

When memory is irrelevant or misleading, these systems degrade silently. This is particularly problematic for deployed systems where retraining is impossible and failures are costly.

1.2 The CAPE Thesis

CAPE proposes a fundamentally different architecture: a frozen base model augmented by a prediction-error-anchored memory that is consulted only when calibrated benefit estimation indicates probable improvement. The core claim is:

CAPE is among the first frameworks to provide formal selective risk control for memory-assisted prediction in embodied agents, improving performance when retrieved memories are informative while provably limiting harmful activation—all without modifying the base world model or policy.

This claim is grounded in three technical commitments:

1. Memory as optional intervention: Memory is not always injected; the system decides when to use it and when to abstain, with abstention guaranteeing exact recovery of the base predictor.
2. Predictive benefit as the objective: Retrieval and gating optimize expected reduction in decision loss, not visual similarity or information-theoretic relevance alone.
3. Calibrated selective risk control: A conformal risk control mechanism provides finite-sample guarantees on the expected loss increase among activated memories.

The conceptual heart of CAPE is the recognition that memory is not knowledge; memory is evidence. The robot does not merely remember—it asks: "Does this memory deserve to change what I currently believe will happen?" Formally, CAPE embodies the decision process:

\text{Historical experience} \;\to\; \text{candidate intervention} \;\to\; \text{estimated benefit} \;\to\; \text{calibrated decision} \;\to\; \begin{cases} \text{intervene}\\ \text{abstain} \end{cases}

A memory is not a fact; it is a single data point from a stochastic physical process. CAPE treats it as statistical evidence whose weight depends on its estimated relevance and the reliability of the estimator. The gate is the robot's skepticism.

1.3 Contributions

The primary contributions of this thesis are:

1. Formal definition of the Selective Predictive Memory (SPM) problem as a constrained optimization over a memory policy with explicit benefit estimation, risk control, and abstention.
2. Decision-theoretic reformulation that replaces mutual information as the primary objective with expected predictive benefit, defined as the reduction in decision loss from memory use.
3. Three-layer theoretical hierarchy providing:
   · (A) finite-sample selective risk control via conformal risk control;
   · (B) prediction-error propagation under contraction; and
   · (C) closed-loop control cost bounds under prediction-Lipschitz controller sensitivity.
4. Architectural specification with two-tier retrieval, unified benefit gating for all memory candidates, lightweight correction (relative capacity constraints), learned benefit estimation with explicit exploration to counter selection bias, and conformal calibration with isolated data buffers.
5. Experimental protocol benchmarking against state-of-the-art systems with real-robot validation, adversarial stress tests, distribution shift detection, calibration analysis, and a causal ablation design isolating the contributions of retrieval, benefit estimation, and calibrated abstention.

1.4 Positioning

The recent literature demonstrates that memory can help robots. CAPE's question is different: Can a frozen embodied predictor selectively use historical physical prediction errors only when doing so has sufficiently high expected utility, while retaining a mathematically controlled fallback to the base predictor?

