Chapter 2: Related Work

2.1 Memory-Augmented World Models

Several recent works augment world models with external memory to improve long-horizon prediction. Mem-World introduces a 4D surfel-indexed memory for action-conditioned world models, achieving persistent manipulation rollouts under occlusion. MemoryVAM augments video world models with episodic memory via a Recap-Cue module. Both co-train memory with the world model and retrieve based on geometric or visual similarity. CAPE differs by operating as a frozen middleware layer and retrieving based on estimated predictive benefit.

2.2 Memory-Augmented Policies

MemoryVLA proposes a Perceptual-Cognitive Memory Bank with working and episodic memory, achieving strong results on the LIBERO benchmark. MemoryVLA++ extends this with world-model imagination. Chameleon introduces bio-inspired hierarchical memory. These systems co-train memory with the VLA backbone and do not provide abstention mechanisms or risk guarantees.

2.3 Test-Time Compute Scaling

τ₀-VLA demonstrates adaptive world-model-guided test-time computation with a confidence router. CAPE is complementary: τ₀-VLA scales reasoning when uncertain; CAPE retrieves memory when beneficial. The two could be combined, but CAPE's contribution is memory, not reasoning depth.

2.4 Uncertainty Estimation and Calibration

RWM-U demonstrates uncertainty-aware robotic world models on real robots using ensemble-based epistemic uncertainty. Foresight uses action-conditioned world-model latents for runtime failure detection. CAPE builds on these by adding a calibrated gating mechanism for memory use with finite-sample selective risk guarantees.

2.5 Biological Memory Models

D-MEM proposes dopamine-gated memory via Reward Prediction Error (RPE) routing. Surprise-Gated Robot Episodic Memory uses prediction error to gate memory writing. Both use prediction error as a gating signal, but neither formalizes retrieval as benefit estimation nor provides risk control guarantees.

2.6 Selective Prediction and Risk Control

The literature on selective prediction (El-Yaniv & Wiener, 2010; Geifman & El-Yaniv, 2017) and conformal prediction (Vovk et al., 2005) provides the mathematical foundation for CAPE's abstention mechanism. Conformal risk control (Angelopoulos et al., 2024) extends conformal prediction to control the expected loss on selected predictions, which is exactly the formal tool needed for memory gating. The novelty of CAPE is the application of selective risk control to memory retrieval in embodied prediction with physical error signals.

