Chapter 3: Problem Formulation

3.1 Environment and Agent

Consider an embodied agent interacting with a stochastic environment:

s_{t+1} = F(s_t, a_t, \xi_t),

where s_t \in \mathcal{S} is the latent physical state, a_t \in \mathcal{A} is the action, and \xi_t is a disturbance. The agent receives observations o_t = H(s_t, \eta_t) with noise \eta_t. It maintains an estimated state \hat{s}_t and a base world model W_\theta:

\hat{s}_{t+1}^{\mathrm{base}} = W_\theta(\hat{s}_t, a_t).

Critical constraint: The base model W_\theta and policy \pi are frozen during CAPE operation. Only the memory, retrieval, benefit estimation, correction, and calibration modules adapt online.

3.2 Memory Representation

A memory entry e_i is a structured tuple:

e_i = \left( c_i, g_i, \mathbf{s}_i, \mathbf{a}_i, \mathbf{o}_i, \mathbf{f}_i, \hat{\mathbf{s}}_i^{\mathrm{base}}, \mathbf{s}^{\mathrm{obs}}_i, \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_i, u_i, r_i \right),

where:

· c_i: context encoding (scene features, object properties)
· g_i: goal encoding
· \mathbf{s}_i: estimated state trajectory over a window
· \mathbf{a}_i: action trajectory
· \mathbf{o}_i: observation trajectory (multimodal)
· \mathbf{f}_i: force/tactile signals (optional)
· \hat{\mathbf{s}}_i^{\mathrm{base}}: state trajectory predicted by the frozen base model
· \mathbf{s}^{\mathrm{obs}}_i: actually observed state trajectory
· \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_i = \mathbf{s}^{\mathrm{obs}}_i - \hat{\mathbf{s}}_i^{\mathrm{base}}: prediction error trajectory
· u_i: uncertainty estimate from the base model at prediction time
· r_i: outcome/reward/success indicator

The prediction error \boldsymbol{\epsilon}_i is the central content of memory, encoding systematic discrepancies between model expectation and physical reality.

3.3 Query and Retrieval

At time t, the agent constructs a query:

q_t = (\hat{s}_t, g_t, a_t^{\mathrm{candidate}}, c_t, f_t, u_t).

Two-Tier Retrieval Architecture:

· Tier 1 (Working Memory Buffer): A small cache of the K=16 most recently used memories. If the maximum cosine similarity between the current query and any cached memory exceeds \tau_{\mathrm{wm}}, the cached memory is taken as a candidate, but it must still pass the benefit gate (Section 3.4–3.5).
· Tier 2 (Long-Term Memory Search): If Tier 1 fails, perform approximate nearest neighbor search on the full memory bank using a FAISS index over context encodings c_i. Retrieve the top-M=50 candidates.
· Tier 3 (Benefit Scoring): Score candidates using the learned benefit estimator \widehat{\Delta}_\psi(q_t, e_i). Among the candidate set \mathcal{R}_t (either a single working-memory hit or the top-M long-term candidates), the selected memory is:

e_t^* = \arg\max_{e \in \mathcal{R}_t} \widehat{\Delta}_\psi(q_t, e).

3.4 Predictive Benefit

Definition 1 (Base Predictive Loss).

L_t^0 = \ell\left( s_{t+1}, W_\theta(\hat{s}_t, a_t) \right),

where \ell is a loss function (e.g., squared error, task-specific cost).

Definition 2 (Memory-Conditioned Loss).

L_t(e) = \ell\left( s_{t+1}, W_\theta(\hat{s}_t, a_t) + \Delta W_\psi(\hat{s}_t, a_t, \phi(e)) \right),

where \Delta W_\psi is the correction network and \phi(e) is the memory encoder.

Definition 3 (Memory Benefit).

\Delta_t(e) = L_t^0 - L_t(e).

This is the core quantity CAPE seeks to predict and optimize. \Delta_t(e) > 0 means memory helped; \Delta_t(e) < 0 means memory harmed the prediction.

Definition 4 (Expected Predictive Benefit).

U(q, e) = \mathbb{E}\left[ \Delta_t(e) \mid q_t = q, e_i = e \right].

This is the theoretical target of the benefit estimator \widehat{\Delta}_\psi.

3.5 Selective Memory Policy and Gate

For a selected memory e_t^*, the gate is defined using the lower confidence bound of the estimated benefit:

L^\Delta(q_t, e_t^*) = \widehat{\Delta}_\psi(q_t, e_t^*) - \kappa,

where \kappa is a calibrated threshold (constant across queries in the basic formulation; query-dependent extensions are discussed in Remark 1). The activation decision is:

G_t = \mathbf{1}\left[ L^\Delta(q_t, e_t^*) > 0 \right].

The final prediction is:

\hat{s}_{t+1}^{\mathrm{CAPE}} = \begin{cases}
W_\theta(\hat{s}_t, a_t) + \Delta W_\psi(\hat{s}_t, a_t, \phi(e_t^*)) & \text{if } G_t = 1,\\
W_\theta(\hat{s}_t, a_t) & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

Parameter Budget: \Delta W_\psi is a 2-layer MLP with hidden dimension d \in \{64, 128, 256, 512\} (swept in experiments). The constraint is relative capacity:

\frac{\text{params}(\Delta W_\psi)}{\text{params}(W_\theta)} \le 10^{-3}.

3.6 The Selective Predictive Memory Problem

Definition 5 (Selective Predictive Memory Problem). Given a query q_t, retrieval set \mathcal{R}_t, and memory bank \mathcal{M}, the SPM problem is to select a memory e_t^* \in \mathcal{R}_t \cup \{\varnothing\} and activation decision G_t \in \{0,1\} that minimizes expected loss subject to a risk constraint:

\min_{\pi_M} \mathbb{E}\left[ \ell_t^{\mathrm{CAPE}} \right]

subject to:

\mathbb{E}\left[ \ell_t^{\mathrm{CAPE}} - \ell_t^{\mathrm{base}} \mid G_t = 1 \right] \le \varepsilon,

and:

\mathbb{E}\left[ \text{compute}_t \right] \le C_{\max},

where \pi_M: q_t \rightarrow (e_t^*, G_t) is the memory policy, \varepsilon > 0 is the maximum allowed selective risk, and C_{\max} is a computational budget.

Key Property (Zero-Cost Abstention): If G_t = 0, then \hat{s}_{t+1}^{\mathrm{CAPE}} = \hat{s}_{t+1}^{\mathrm{base}}, and therefore \ell_t^{\mathrm{CAPE}} = \ell_t^{\mathrm{base}} exactly. This is an architectural guarantee, not a statistical one.

