5.2 Pseudocode

```
Initialize base world model W (pretrained, frozen)
Initialize correction network ΔW (2-layer MLP, hidden dim sweep)
Initialize benefit estimator Δ̂ (small MLP)
Initialize memory M (empty)
Initialize working memory buffer B (size K=16)
Initialize calibration buffer D_cal (isolated from training)
Initialize training buffer D_train
Initialize target network V_ξ^target = V_ξ

# Exploration schedule
ε_explore = 0.10

For each episode:
    For t = 0 to T:
        Observe o_t, update state estimate ŝ_t
        Construct query q_t
        
        # Tier 1: Working memory
        if B contains memory e with cosine_sim(c(q_t), c(e)) > τ_wm:
            candidate = e
        else:
            # Tier 2: Long-term memory search
            R = FAISS_search(M, c_t, top_M=50)
            # Tier 3: Benefit scoring
            candidate = argmax_{e in R} Δ̂(q_t, e)
        
        # Unified gate for ALL candidates
        L^Δ = Δ̂(q_t, candidate) - κ   # κ calibrated offline
        
        # ε-greedy exploration to counter selection bias
        if random() < ε_explore:
            G_t = 1
            explore_flag = True
        else:
            G_t = 1[L^Δ > 0]
            explore_flag = False
        
        if G_t == 1:
            m_t = φ(candidate)
            ŝ_{t+1}^CAPE = W(ŝ_t, a_t) + ΔW(ŝ_t, a_t, m_t)
        else:
            m_t = 0
            ŝ_{t+1}^CAPE = W(ŝ_t, a_t)
        
        # Plan and execute
        Select action a_t using policy π(ŝ_t, g_t, m_t)
        Execute a_t, observe o_{t+1}, compute ŝ_{t+1}^obs
        
        # Compute losses
        L_t^0 = ℓ(ŝ_{t+1}^obs, W(ŝ_t, a_t))
        L_t^CAPE = ℓ(ŝ_{t+1}^obs, ŝ_{t+1}^CAPE)
        Δ_t = L_t^0 - L_t^CAPE
        
        # Update working memory buffer
        B.push((q_t, candidate, Δ_t))
        
        # Update calibration buffer (only after freeze of Δ̂ and ΔW)
        if t % T_cal == 0:
            D_cal.push((q_t, candidate, Δ_t))
            recalibrate κ via conformal risk control on D_cal
        
        # Construct experience
        e_t = (c_t, g_t, ŝ_t, a_t, o_t, f_t, ŝ_t^base, ŝ_t^obs, ε_t^base, u_t, r_t)
        
        # Learned consolidation (TD learning with target network)
        if t % T_update == 0:
            # Update correction network
            Update ΔW via L_ΔW = ||ε_t^CAPE||²
            
            # Update benefit estimator (on ALL activations, including exploration)
            if G_t == 1:
                Update Δ̂ via L_Δ̂ = (Δ_t - Δ̂(q_t, candidate))²
            
            # Update consolidation value network (online TD)
            if t < T-1:
                y_t = Δ_t + γ * V_ξ^target(e_{t+1})
            else:
                y_t = Δ_t  # terminal
            Update V_ξ via L_V = (V_ξ(e_t) - y_t)²
            
            # Soft-update target network
            V_ξ^target ← τ * V_ξ + (1-τ) * V_ξ^target
        
        # Insert into long-term memory
        if V_ξ(e_t) > threshold:
            M.insert(e_t)
            if |M| > N_max:
                evict argmin_i V_ξ(e_i)
    
    # Episode-end: anneal exploration
    ε_explore ← max(0.01, ε_explore * 0.995)
```

5.3 Data Isolation Protocol

To maintain the validity of the calibration guarantee, CAPE uses three isolated data buffers:

Buffer Purpose Used For
\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{train}} Training the correction network \Delta W_\psi, benefit estimator \widehat{\Delta}_\psi, and consolidation network V_\xi Gradient updates
\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{cal}} Calibrating the threshold \kappa Conformal risk control
\mathcal{D}_{\mathrm{test}} Final evaluation Reporting results

Three-Timescale Architecture:

· Fast: Retrieval and gating (every step)
· Medium: Model updates (\Delta W_\psi, \widehat{\Delta}_\psi, V_\xi) every T_{\mathrm{update}} steps
· Slow: Calibration refresh every T_{\mathrm{cal}} steps, where T_{\mathrm{cal}} \gg T_{\mathrm{update}}

5.4 Conformal Calibration

The calibration procedure follows the conformal risk control approach described in Section 4.1. The threshold \kappa is selected from a finite candidate set \mathcal{K} (e.g., a grid over [-L_{\max}, L_{\max}] with 100 values) by minimizing the empirical selective risk \widehat{R}(\kappa) subject to the constraint \widehat{R}(\kappa) \le \varepsilon.

Continuous thresholds. If a continuous threshold is desired, one may use the randomized selection rule from Angelopoulos et al. (2024): let \kappa^- and \kappa^+ be the two grid points bracketing the target risk level, and randomize between them with probability chosen to interpolate the empirical risk exactly to \varepsilon. This yields an exact expectation guarantee even for continuous thresholds without requiring an infinite grid.

5.5 Memory Encoder \phi

The encoder \phi(e_i) extracts a vector capturing the essential predictive information:

\phi(e_i) = \text{MLP}_\phi\left( c_i, g_i, \bar{\epsilon}_i, \Sigma_{\epsilon_i}, \text{last\_action}(a_i), \text{last\_state}(\hat{s}_i) \right),

where \bar{\epsilon}_i is the mean prediction error over the window and \Sigma_{\epsilon_i} is the covariance. The covariance is regularized by adding a small diagonal term \lambda I with \lambda = 10^{-4} to ensure numerical stability. The window length for computing \bar{\epsilon}_i and \Sigma_{\epsilon_i} is a hyperparameter, typically 10–50 steps.

5.6 Benefit Estimator \widehat{\Delta}_\psi

The benefit estimator is trained to predict the actual loss reduction from using a specific memory:

\mathcal{L}_{\widehat{\Delta}} = \left( \Delta_t - \widehat{\Delta}_\psi(q_t, e_t^*) \right)^2,

where \Delta_t = L_t^0 - L_t^{\mathrm{CAPE}} is observed after execution.

Critical: Exploration for unbiased training. Because \Delta_t is only observed when G_t = 1, the training data for \widehat{\Delta}_\psi is subject to positive selection bias: the estimator sees only memories that passed the gate (or were forced via exploration). Without negative examples, the estimator would systematically overestimate benefit for borderline candidates. The \varepsilon-greedy mechanism in the pseudocode ensures that a fraction \varepsilon_{\text{explore}} of activations are forced regardless of the gate, providing unbiased training data for harmful or neutral memories. We anneal \varepsilon_{\text{explore}} from 0.10 to 0.01 over the course of training.

Remark 3 (Bias-variance tradeoff of exploration). Higher \varepsilon_{\text{explore}} reduces selection bias but may degrade performance during training by activating harmful memories more often. The annealing schedule balances this tradeoff: early exploration gathers diverse training data, while later exploitation allows the system to benefit from learned improvements.

5.7 Learned Memory Consolidation

A memory value network V_\xi predicts the expected future benefit if e_t is retained. To avoid requiring future information during online training, we use temporal difference (TD) learning with a slowly updated target network:

y_t = \Delta_t + \gamma V_\xi^{\text{target}}(e_{t+1}),

\mathcal{L}_V = \left( V_\xi(e_t) - y_t \right)^2.

The target network V_\xi^{\text{target}} is soft-updated via Polyak averaging with coefficient \tau \in [0.001, 0.01]. When memory is full, the entry with the lowest V_\xi(e_i) is evicted.

5.8 Computational Complexity

The per-step computational overhead of CAPE is summarized below, demonstrating compatibility with real-time robot control (target: 10–50 Hz).

Operation Complexity Typical Latency
Tier 1: Working memory scan O(K), K=16 <0.1 ms
Tier 2: FAISS IVF search O(\log N_{\max}) <1 ms
Tier 3: Benefit scoring (M=50 candidates) O(M \cdot d^2) <2 ms
Gate evaluation O(1) <0.1 ms
Correction network forward pass O(d^2) <1 ms
Total overhead  <5 ms

The total inference overhead is negligible compared to the base world model forward pass (typically 20–50 ms for a 1B-parameter model). Memory insertion and consolidation occur asynchronously and do not block the control loop.

