Appendix B: Full Proofs of Error Propagation Lemmas

B.1 Proof of Lemma 1 (Error Propagation)

Lemma 1 (Restated).
Under Assumptions 2 (Lipschitz dynamics) and 3 (Stable MPC), for horizon H,

\|s_{t+H} - \hat{s}_{t+H}\| \le \gamma^H \|s_t - \hat{s}_t\| + \sum_{k=0}^{H-1} \gamma^{H-1-k} \|\epsilon_{t+k}\|.

Proof.
We proceed by induction on H.

Base case H=1:
Assumption 3 directly gives

\|s_{t+1} - \hat{s}_{t+1}\| \le \gamma \|s_t - \hat{s}_t\| + \|\epsilon_t\|.

This is exactly the formula for H=1.

Induction step:
Assume the inequality holds for some H \ge 1. We prove it for H+1. By Assumption 3 applied at time t+H,

\|s_{t+H+1} - \hat{s}_{t+H+1}\|
\le \gamma \|s_{t+H} - \hat{s}_{t+H}\| + \|\epsilon_{t+H}\|.

Substitute the induction hypothesis for \|s_{t+H} - \hat{s}_{t+H}\|:

\|s_{t+H+1} - \hat{s}_{t+H+1}\|
\le \gamma \left( \gamma^H \|s_t - \hat{s}_t\| + \sum_{k=0}^{H-1} \gamma^{H-1-k} \|\epsilon_{t+k}\| \right) + \|\epsilon_{t+H}\|.

Distribute \gamma:

= \gamma^{H+1} \|s_t - \hat{s}_t\| + \sum_{k=0}^{H-1} \gamma^{H-k} \|\epsilon_{t+k}\| + \|\epsilon_{t+H}\|.

The last term \|\epsilon_{t+H}\| can be included in the sum by extending the upper limit:

\sum_{k=0}^{H} \gamma^{H-k} \|\epsilon_{t+k}\|

with the convention that \gamma^{0}=1. Thus,

\|s_{t+H+1} - \hat{s}_{t+H+1}\|
\le \gamma^{H+1} \|s_t - \hat{s}_t\| + \sum_{k=0}^{H} \gamma^{H-k} \|\epsilon_{t+k}\|.

This matches the formula for horizon H+1. By induction, the lemma holds for all H \ge 1. \blacksquare

B.2 Proof of Lemma 2 (Selective Error Reduction)

Lemma 2 (Restated).
Suppose that for steps where G_{t+k}=1,

\mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{CAPE}}\| \mid G_{t+k}=1 \right] \le (1-\alpha) \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \right],

for some \alpha > 0. Then

\mathbb{E}\left[ \|s_{t+H} - \hat{s}_{t+H}^{\mathrm{CAPE}}\| \right] \le \mathbb{E}\left[ \|s_{t+H} - \hat{s}_{t+H}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \right] - \alpha \sum_{k=0}^{H-1} \gamma^{H-1-k} \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \right] P(G_{t+k}=1).

Proof.
We first derive a bound on the expected one-step error of CAPE relative to the baseline.

For each k, by the law of total expectation,

\mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{CAPE}}\| \right]
= \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{CAPE}}\| \mid G_{t+k}=1 \right] P(G_{t+k}=1)
+ \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{CAPE}}\| \mid G_{t+k}=0 \right] P(G_{t+k}=0).

When G_{t+k}=0, CAPE abstains, so \epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{CAPE}} = \epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}. Thus,

\mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{CAPE}}\| \mid G_{t+k}=0 \right]
= \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \mid G_{t+k}=0 \right].

Using the selective error reduction condition,

\mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{CAPE}}\| \mid G_{t+k}=1 \right]
\le (1-\alpha) \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \right].

Therefore,

\mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{CAPE}}\| \right]
\le (1-\alpha) \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \right] P(G_{t+k}=1)
+ \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \mid G_{t+k}=0 \right] P(G_{t+k}=0).

We compare this to the baseline unconditional expectation:

\mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \right]
= \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \mid G_{t+k}=1 \right] P(G_{t+k}=1)
+ \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \mid G_{t+k}=0 \right] P(G_{t+k}=0).

Subtracting the CAPE bound from the baseline expectation,

\mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \right] - \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{CAPE}}\| \right]
\ge \left( \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \mid G_{t+k}=1 \right] - (1-\alpha) \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \right] \right) P(G_{t+k}=1).

To simplify, we make the mild assumption that \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \mid G_{t+k}=1 \right] \ge \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \right] (i.e., the baseline error is at least as large on activated steps as on average). This is plausible because the gate is more likely to activate in situations of higher prediction difficulty. Under this assumption, the reduction is at least

\alpha \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \right] P(G_{t+k}=1).

Thus,

\mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{CAPE}}\| \right]
\le \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \right] - \alpha \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \right] P(G_{t+k}=1).

Now, apply Lemma 1 to the CAPE system:

\mathbb{E}\left[ \|s_{t+H} - \hat{s}_{t+H}^{\mathrm{CAPE}}\| \right]
\le \gamma^H \mathbb{E}\left[ \|s_t - \hat{s}_t\| \right]
+ \sum_{k=0}^{H-1} \gamma^{H-1-k} \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{CAPE}}\| \right].

Substitute the upper bound for \mathbb{E}[\|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{CAPE}}\|]:

\mathbb{E}\left[ \|s_{t+H} - \hat{s}_{t+H}^{\mathrm{CAPE}}\| \right]
\le \gamma^H \mathbb{E}\left[ \|s_t - \hat{s}_t\| \right]
+ \sum_{k=0}^{H-1} \gamma^{H-1-k} \left( \mathbb{E}[\|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\|] - \alpha \mathbb{E}[\|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\|] P(G_{t+k}=1) \right).

For the baseline system, Lemma 1 gives

\mathbb{E}\left[ \|s_{t+H} - \hat{s}_{t+H}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \right]
\le \gamma^H \mathbb{E}\left[ \|s_t - \hat{s}_t\| \right]
+ \sum_{k=0}^{H-1} \gamma^{H-1-k} \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \right].

Subtracting the CAPE bound from the baseline bound,

\mathbb{E}\left[ \|s_{t+H} - \hat{s}_{t+H}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \right]
- \mathbb{E}\left[ \|s_{t+H} - \hat{s}_{t+H}^{\mathrm{CAPE}}\| \right]
\ge \alpha \sum_{k=0}^{H-1} \gamma^{H-1-k} \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \right] P(G_{t+k}=1).

Rearranging gives exactly the statement of Lemma 2. \blacksquare

