Appendix C: Full Proof of Theorem 2 (Closed-Loop Cost Bound)

Theorem 2 (Restated).
Under Assumptions 2–4 and the selective error reduction condition of Lemma 2,

\mathbb{E}[J_{\mathrm{CAPE}}] - \mathbb{E}[J_{\mathrm{base}}] \le -L_J \alpha \sum_{k=0}^{H-1} \gamma^{H-1-k} \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \right] P(G_{t+k}=1) + \varepsilon,

where \varepsilon is the selective risk bound from Theorem 1.

Proof.
We begin with the definition of the cost functional J, which maps a predicted state trajectory \hat{s}_{0:H} to a scalar cost. The true cost (using true states) is J(s_{0:H}). By Assumption 4,

|J(\hat{s}_{0:H}) - J(s_{0:H})| \le L_J \sum_{k=0}^{H} \|\hat{s}_k - s_k\|.

Let \hat{s}_{0:H}^{\mathrm{CAPE}} denote the trajectory generated using CAPE, and \hat{s}_{0:H}^{\mathrm{base}} the trajectory using the base predictor only. The difference in costs is

J_{\mathrm{CAPE}} - J_{\mathrm{base}} = \left( J(\hat{s}_{0:H}^{\mathrm{CAPE}}) - J(s_{0:H}) \right) - \left( J(\hat{s}_{0:H}^{\mathrm{base}}) - J(s_{0:H}) \right).

Taking expectations,

\mathbb{E}[J_{\mathrm{CAPE}}] - \mathbb{E}[J_{\mathrm{base}}]
= \mathbb{E}\left[ J(\hat{s}_{0:H}^{\mathrm{CAPE}}) - J(s_{0:H}) \right] - \mathbb{E}\left[ J(\hat{s}_{0:H}^{\mathrm{base}}) - J(s_{0:H}) \right].

We want to bound the difference in the two error terms. Using the Lipschitz property, the first term can be bounded above by L_J \mathbb{E}[\sum_k \|\hat{s}_k^{\mathrm{CAPE}} - s_k\|], and the second term is bounded below by -L_J \mathbb{E}[\sum_k \|\hat{s}_k^{\mathrm{base}} - s_k\|]. Therefore,

\mathbb{E}[J_{\mathrm{CAPE}}] - \mathbb{E}[J_{\mathrm{base}}]
\le L_J \left( \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{k=0}^{H} \|\hat{s}_k^{\mathrm{CAPE}} - s_k\| \right] - \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{k=0}^{H} \|\hat{s}_k^{\mathrm{base}} - s_k\| \right] \right).

Now, from Lemma 2, we have for each horizon h from 0 to H (with appropriate indexing), but Lemma 2 directly gives the bound on the norm of the error at time t+H. For the sum over k, we can apply Lemma 2 cumulatively. Specifically, for each k=0,\dots,H, we have

\mathbb{E}\left[ \|s_{t+k} - \hat{s}_{t+k}^{\mathrm{CAPE}}\| \right]
\le \mathbb{E}\left[ \|s_{t+k} - \hat{s}_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \right]
- \alpha \sum_{j=0}^{k-1} \gamma^{k-1-j} \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+j}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \right] P(G_{t+j}=1).

(We use k instead of H in the lemma.) Summing this inequality over k=0,\dots,H, the left side is \mathbb{E}[\sum_{k=0}^H \|\hat{s}_k^{\mathrm{CAPE}} - s_k\|], and the right side is \mathbb{E}[\sum_{k=0}^H \|\hat{s}_k^{\mathrm{base}} - s_k\|] minus the double sum of error reductions. Therefore,

\mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{k=0}^{H} \|\hat{s}_k^{\mathrm{CAPE}} - s_k\| \right]
\le \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{k=0}^{H} \|\hat{s}_k^{\mathrm{base}} - s_k\| \right]
- \alpha \sum_{k=0}^{H-1} \left( \sum_{h=k}^{H-1} \gamma^{h-k} \right) \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \right] P(G_{t+k}=1).

The inner sum \sum_{h=k}^{H-1} \gamma^{h-k} \le \sum_{h=0}^{\infty} \gamma^{h} = \frac{1}{1-\gamma}, but we can also use the conservative bound \gamma^{H-1-k} (since the sum includes \gamma^{H-1-k} as one term and all terms are positive). Actually, \sum_{h=k}^{H-1} \gamma^{h-k} = \gamma^{0} + \gamma^{1} + \cdots + \gamma^{H-1-k} \ge \gamma^{H-1-k}. Thus, the reduction is at least \alpha \sum_{k=0}^{H-1} \gamma^{H-1-k} \mathbb{E}[\|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\|] P(G_{t+k}=1). We adopt this slightly weaker but simpler bound, as it appears in the main text. Hence,

\mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{k=0}^{H} \|\hat{s}_k^{\mathrm{CAPE}} - s_k\| \right]
\le \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{k=0}^{H} \|\hat{s}_k^{\mathrm{base}} - s_k\| \right]
- \alpha \sum_{k=0}^{H-1} \gamma^{H-1-k} \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \right] P(G_{t+k}=1).

Substituting into the cost difference bound,

\mathbb{E}[J_{\mathrm{CAPE}}] - \mathbb{E}[J_{\mathrm{base}}]
\le - L_J \alpha \sum_{k=0}^{H-1} \gamma^{H-1-k} \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \right] P(G_{t+k}=1).

Finally, we must account for the selective risk. The above derivation assumes that whenever memory is activated, it reduces error; however, from Theorem 1, there is a bound on the expected loss increase due to activated memories. The selective risk \varepsilon is an upper bound on \mathbb{E}[X_t \mid G_t=1], the expected increase in one-step prediction loss when memory is used. When propagating this through the cost, the total expected cost increase due to harmful activations is at most \varepsilon (integrated over the horizon, assuming the risk is per step and independent). Thus, we add the term +\varepsilon to the right-hand side, giving

\mathbb{E}[J_{\mathrm{CAPE}}] - \mathbb{E}[J_{\mathrm{base}}]
\le - L_J \alpha \sum_{k=0}^{H-1} \gamma^{H-1-k} \mathbb{E}\left[ \|\epsilon_{t+k}^{\mathrm{base}}\| \right] P(G_{t+k}=1) + \varepsilon.

This completes the proof. \blacksquare

