Appendix E: Benefit Estimator Training and Exploration Bias Analysis (Theoretical)

E.1 Training Objective and Selection Bias

The benefit estimator \widehat{\Delta}_\psi(q,e) aims to predict the true expected benefit \mathbb{E}[\Delta \mid q,e]. Ideally, it would be trained on a dataset of triplets (q,e,\Delta) drawn i.i.d. from the natural distribution of queries and candidate memories. However, in CAPE, \Delta is only observed when memory is activated, i.e., when G=1. Thus, the training set consists of (q,e,\Delta) where G=1, which induces a selection bias.

Let p(q,e) be the natural joint distribution of queries and candidate memories. The selection probability under a fixed gate and exploration policy is p_{\mathrm{sel}}(q,e) = \mathbb{P}(G=1 \mid q,e). The training distribution is proportional to p_{\mathrm{train}}(q,e) \propto p_{\mathrm{sel}}(q,e) p(q,e). If the gate is deterministic and based on \widehat{\Delta}, then p_{\mathrm{sel}}(q,e) is 1 for memories with high estimated benefit and 0 otherwise, severely truncating the distribution.

E.2 Unbiased Estimation via Exploration

To counteract this bias, CAPE uses \varepsilon-greedy exploration: with probability \varepsilon_{\text{explore}}, the gate is forced to activate regardless of estimated benefit. This yields the effective training distribution

p_{\mathrm{train}}(q,e) \propto \left[ (1-\varepsilon_{\text{explore}}) p_{\mathrm{sel}}(q,e) + \varepsilon_{\text{explore}} \right] p(q,e).

As \varepsilon_{\text{explore}} \to 1, the training distribution approaches the natural distribution, eliminating bias. However, a large \varepsilon_{\text{explore}} leads to many harmful activations during training, degrading performance. The annealing schedule from 0.10 to 0.01 balances exploration and exploitation.

E.3 Bias Analysis

Let f(q,e) = \mathbb{E}[\Delta \mid q,e] be the true benefit function. The biased estimator trained on data with selection probability p_{\mathrm{sel}} converges (in the limit of infinite data) to the function

\tilde{f}(q,e) = \mathbb{E}[\Delta \mid q,e, G=1] = f(q,e) + \text{bias}(q,e),

where

\text{bias}(q,e) = \mathbb{E}[\Delta \mid q,e, G=1] - f(q,e).

Under the gate G=1 iff \widehat{\Delta}(q,e) - \kappa > 0, the bias is positive for borderline memories: those for which \widehat{\Delta} is near \kappa and the observed \Delta happens to be high enough to pass. This is a form of "winner's curse". Exploration reduces this bias by including cases where the gate would not fire but the memory is activated anyway, thus observing the true (possibly negative) benefit.

E.4 Effect on Calibration

The benefit estimator is used to compute the lower confidence bound L^\Delta = \widehat{\Delta} - \kappa. If \widehat{\Delta} is biased upward, the gate will activate more often than it should, increasing selective risk. The conformal risk control calibration on a separate, unbiased calibration set (collected with exploration) adjusts the threshold \kappa to compensate, ensuring that the selective risk bound still holds. This is the reason for the isolated calibration buffer: it should contain data collected under a fixed exploration policy, so that the empirical risk reflects the true distribution of future activations.

