Appendix G: Computational Complexity Analysis (Theoretical)

We analyze the computational cost of CAPE at inference time, assuming a GPU-accelerated implementation.

G.1 Per-Step Complexity

· Working memory scan: Iterates over K=16 entries, computing cosine similarity between context encodings of dimension d_c. Complexity O(K d_c). With d_c=512, this is about 8192 operations, negligible.
· Long-term memory search (FAISS): Using an inverted file index with n_{\text{probe}} probes, the search cost is approximately O(n_{\text{probe}} \log N_{\max}) distance computations. With n_{\text{probe}}=8, N_{\max}=10^4, this is ~100 distance computations of dimension d_c.
· Benefit scoring: For M=50 candidates, a forward pass through a small MLP \widehat{\Delta}_\psi with hidden dimension d=128. Complexity O(M d^2). With d=128, this is ~800k multiply-adds, well under 1 ms on GPU.
· Gate evaluation: O(1).
· Correction network forward pass: One forward pass through a 2-layer MLP with hidden dim d=128. Complexity O(d^2), similar to above.

Total overhead: well under 5 ms on modern GPUs, compatible with real-time control at 50 Hz or more.

G.2 Memory Footprint

· Working buffer: K \times size of one memory entry. Each entry includes context encoding (512 floats), state/action trajectories (e.g., 10 steps × 20 dims each), prediction errors, etc. Total maybe a few KB per entry, so working buffer ~64 KB.
· Long-term memory: N_{\max} entries. With N_{\max}=10^4, each entry on the order of 10–100 KB (depending on trajectory length), total 100 MB–1 GB.
· FAISS index: ~100 MB for 10k vectors of dimension 512.

This is feasible for embedded systems with modest RAM (a few GB).

G.3 Asynchronous Operations

Memory insertion, consolidation updates, and calibration refresh are performed asynchronously on a separate thread or process, so they do not block the control loop.

