Appendix I: Additional Lemmas and Theoretical Discussion

I.1 Local Lipschitzness of Neural Networks

Lemma I.1. A feedforward neural network with Lipschitz activation functions (e.g., ReLU, tanh) and bounded weights is locally Lipschitz continuous.

Proof. By Rademacher's theorem, a locally Lipschitz function is differentiable almost everywhere, and its Lipschitz constant on a compact set is bounded by the supremum of the norm of its gradient over that set. For a neural network, the gradient is bounded if the weights are bounded and the activation derivatives are bounded. On any compact subset of the input space, the gradient norm is finite, hence the network is locally Lipschitz. \blacksquare

This justifies Assumptions 2 and 4 on compact domains.

I.2 Contraction Property for Stable MPC

Lemma I.2. Consider a linear time-invariant system s_{t+1} = A s_t + B a_t controlled by an MPC policy that minimizes a quadratic cost with positive definite state penalty Q and input penalty R. If (A,B) is stabilizable, then the closed-loop system is asymptotically stable, and the error dynamics satisfy a contraction of the form

\|s_{t+1} - \hat{s}_{t+1}\| \le \gamma \|s_t - \hat{s}_t\| + \|\epsilon_t\|

for some \gamma \in (0,1).

Proof sketch. For linear MPC without constraints, the optimal control law is linear and the closed-loop system is stable. The contraction rate is the spectral radius of the closed-loop matrix A - B K, which is <1 by design. For nonlinear systems, local contraction can be established by linearization around a nominal trajectory and using the implicit function theorem. \blacksquare

I.3 Sequential Conformal Prediction for Closed-Loop Systems

In Section 7.3, we note that closed-loop operation violates exchangeability. Sequential conformal prediction (Gibbs & Candès, 2021) provides a framework for obtaining valid prediction intervals even when the data is generated by an arbitrary (possibly adversarial) process. The key idea is to maintain a time-varying threshold that is updated online using past errors, and to bound the miscoverage rate via online learning regret. For CAPE, this would involve replacing the fixed threshold \kappa with an adaptive \kappa_t that is updated after each step based on the observed loss. This is an important direction for future theoretical work to extend the risk control guarantee to non-exchangeable, closed-loop settings.

